# CVE-2025-59250 (mssql-jdbc): confirmed false positive here, not silently
# suppressing a real vulnerability.
#
# Keycloak 26.7.3 (the version this Dockerfile pins) declares
# <mssql-jdbc.version>13.2.1.jre11</mssql-jdbc.version> in its own pom.xml --
# not the bare "13.2.1" a naive read of the Trivy finding suggests. Microsoft's
# published fixed-version list for this CVE explicitly includes 13.2.1.jre11.
#
# The false positive is a documented, maintainer-acknowledged Trivy/Grype
# defect class for this exact package: mssql-jdbc's shipped JAR embeds its
# version in pom.properties WITHOUT the ".jreNN" suffix ("13.2.1"), even
# though the resolved Maven coordinate (and JAR filename) is "13.2.1.jre11".
# Maven's version-ordering rules judge "13.2.1" < "13.2.1.jre8", so a scanner
# reading pom.properties flags an already-patched build as vulnerable.
# See: https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/discussions/9745
#      https://github.com/anchore/grype/issues/3042
#
# Verified directly against keycloak/keycloak's pom.xml at the 26.7.3 tag
# (docs/governance/gate-iam-0-discovery.md §4.10) since quay.io registry
# access is blocked in this environment (same constraint as R-1). Revisit
# if the pinned Keycloak/mssql-jdbc version changes, or if a future scan
# flags a DIFFERENT CVE for mssql-jdbc -- that would need independent
# verification, not coverage under this entry.
CVE-2025-59250
